                </span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Schema & Backend Handover Notice -->
    <div class="ht-admin-notice-banner notice-warning" role="alert">
        <div class="ht-notice-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="m21.73 18-8-14a2 2 0 0 0-3.48 0l-8 14A2 2 0 0 0 4 21h16a2 2 0 0 0 1.73-3Z"></path>
                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"></line>
                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"></line>
            </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="ht-notice-content">
            <h4>Database Schema Handover: Client-to-Trainer Direct Relationship</h4>
            <p>
                <strong>Current Runtime State:</strong> The current Entity Framework Core schema does not contain a direct foreign key from <code>Client</code> to <code>Trainer</code>.
                Associations only exist indirectly through individual <code>WorkoutPlan</code>, <code>DietPlan</code>, and <code>Appointment</code> records.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-2">
                <strong>Recommended Implementation for Backend Developer:</strong>
                Add a nullable <code>int? trainerId</code> and navigation property <code>Trainer? trainer</code> to <code>Client.cs</code> with <code>DeleteBehavior.SetNull</code> in <code>HealthTrackDbContext.cs</code>, then execute an EF Core migration.
                Once added, wire up <code>public IActionResult TrainerAssignments()</code> and <code>[HttpPost] public IActionResult AssignTrainer(int clientId, int trainerId)</code>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

